<!--
  /showcase — whole screens, not component swatches. Each screen renders
  live under a DIFFERENT theme scope, with its complete source beside it:
  no hidden CSS, no imports, nothing elided. The screens are deliberately
  unalike — the counter-argument to "every SUI site looks the same".
-->
<column class="doc-wide" gap="5">
  <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
  <h1>Showcase</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Two complete screens. What you see on the left is everything the source on
    the right produces — there is no stylesheet behind it, no import list above
    it, and each screen wears a different theme to make the point: the markup
    does not own the look.
  </p>

  <h2>A settings screen — theme: paper</h2>
  <row class="split" align="stretch">
    <column>
      <p class="pane-label">Live</p>
      <column data-theme-preview="paper" sx="radius:lg border:border bg:background fg:foreground overflow:hidden">
        <column gap="4" sx="p:6">
          <h2>Workspace settings</h2>
          <tabs>
            <tab-list>
              <tab>Profile</tab>
              <tab>Billing</tab>
              <tab>Team</tab>
            </tab-list>
            <tab-panel>
              <column gap="3" sx="pt:4">
                <label>Workspace name</label>
                <input value="Acme Robotics" />
                <label>Plan</label>
                <radio-group>
                  <label><input type="radio" name="plan" checked /> Monthly — $12</label>
                  <label><input type="radio" name="plan" /> Yearly — $120</label>
                </radio-group>
                <row gap="3" align="center">
                  <switch></switch>
                  <p sx="text:sm">Email me when a deploy finishes</p>
                </row>
                <separator></separator>
                <row gap="2">
                  <button variant="solid" tone="primary">Save changes</button>
                  <button variant="ghost">Cancel</button>
                </row>
              </column>
            </tab-panel>
            <tab-panel><p sx="pt:4">Billing settings.</p></tab-panel>
            <tab-panel><p sx="pt:4">Team settings.</p></tab-panel>
          </tabs>
        </column>
      </column>
    </column>
    <column>
      <p class="pane-label">Source — all of it</p>
      <code-block code={code.settings}></code-block>
    </column>
  </row>
  <p>
    The tabs are keyboard-complete, the radio group is a fieldset, the switch
    is a real control — none of that is in the source because none of it is
    the author's job. Thirty-five lines, and every line is about the settings.
  </p>

  <h2>An ops dashboard — theme: console</h2>
  <row class="split" align="stretch">
    <column>
      <p class="pane-label">Live</p>
      <column data-theme-preview="console" sx="radius:lg border:border bg:background fg:foreground overflow:hidden">
        <column gap="4" sx="p:6">
          <row gap="3" align="center">
            <h2 sx="grow:1">Fleet</h2>
            <button variant="outline">Export</button>
            <button variant="solid" tone="primary">Deploy all</button>
          </row>
          <grid cols="3" gap="3">
            <card sx="p:4">
              <badge tone="success">healthy</badge>
              <h3>api-gateway</h3>
              <progress value="82" max="100"></progress>
              <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">p99 41 ms · 3 replicas</p>
            </card>
            <card sx="p:4">
              <badge tone="warning">degraded</badge>
              <h3>search-index</h3>
              <progress value="47" max="100"></progress>
              <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">p99 310 ms · rebuilding</p>
            </card>
            <card sx="p:4">
              <badge tone="success">healthy</badge>
              <h3>billing-worker</h3>
              <progress value="94" max="100"></progress>
              <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">queue empty</p>
            </card>
          </grid>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Deploy</th><th>Service</th><th>Status</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>#214</td><td>api-gateway</td><td><badge tone="success">live</badge></td></tr>
              <tr><td>#213</td><td>search-index</td><td><badge tone="warning">rolling</badge></td></tr>
              <tr><td>#212</td><td>billing-worker</td><td><badge tone="muted">superseded</badge></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </column>
      </column>
    </column>
    <column>
      <p class="pane-label">Source — all of it</p>
      <code-block code={code.fleet}></code-block>
    </column>
  </row>
  <p>
    Same language, same forty-eight components, and it reads as a different
    product — because the theme owns the surfaces and the markup only says
    what the screen <em>is</em>. That separation is the whole bet: agents
    write the second column, and the first column is what everyone gets.
  </p>

  <row class="page-nav" gap="2">
    <a class="btn" href="/why">← Why SUI</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/components">Component library →</a>
  </row>
</column>
